WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.600
We never stop hearing about how the internet's
in the cloud.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.100
But really, it's in the ocean.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:12.650
About 300 undersea fiber optic cables are
responsible for 99% of international data

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:13.650
traffic.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.520
It's basically the same way we connect to
each other in a single country, except under

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.460
water instead of underground.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:26.150
They transmit PewDiePie from Europe to America
and they connect stock traders in New York

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.150
and London.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:32.599
And these cables, placed by private companies,
are the backbone of the internet, but if you

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:36.440
held one in your hand it'd be no bigger than
a soda can.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.820
There are just a few layers of protection
from the water, including petroleum jelly

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.239
(yes, your internet is covered in Vaseline).

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:46.370
They're vulnerable to earthquakes,

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:49.890
at least a few times, confused sharks have
bitten them.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.739
But many cables are beneath sea life, because
in some places they go as deep underwater

00:00:54.739 --> 00:00:57.070
as Mount Everest is high.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:02.559
Ships lower a plow that digs a tiny groove
in the ocean floor, lay in the cable, and

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:05.900
it's naturally buried by sand, thanks to the
ocean's current.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:12.720
And that process -- it's both stunningly simple
and mindblowingly complex -- is responsible

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.220
for making the internet a truly global network.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:21.720
It's an idea so audacious and crazy, and you
think that it has to be cutting edge.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.430
And it is.

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:27.880
But it's also been going on for 157 years.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.770
Electric telegraphs have been around for a
long, long time.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:36.520
Experiments in the early 1800s connected two
ends of a garden, using a clock that revealed

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.180
letters, then they moved on to

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.680
two neighborhoods, to help signal trains,
and then multiple cities, thanks to the network

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.710
of railroad lines.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.270
Underwater "submarine cables" were an obvious
next step.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.270
So they played around.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:55.310
Instead of petroleum jelly, the first ones
were coated with exotic tree sap to protect

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:56.480
them from the water.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.520
And though the undersea cables came in spurts
-- one of the first ones was knocked out of

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.020
commission by a fishing boat --

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:07.920
and by 1858 they reached around the Atlantic
and across the world.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.510
And that's how it's kind of gone since, laying
cables that circle the earth's oceans.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:18.150
The cables are unwound from the back of a
ship, sink to the ocean floor, and the world

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:21.849
is connected in speeds measured in milliseconds.

00:02:21.849 --> 00:02:24.739
There are ideas to bring the internet above
sea level.

00:02:24.739 --> 00:02:27.480
Along with cell phone towers, there's internet
beamed from

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.999
Facebook satellites to Africa and balloons
lifted by Google.

00:02:30.999 --> 00:02:36.590
But for speedy international travel, undersea
cables are still where companies like Facebook

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.990
and Google place their bets.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.719
That's because the best way to create the
cloud is still by going under the sea.

